# test_ball · a ball on a grooved plinth, a flat slab and a small cube: what a surface looks like
# test_ball(surface, at) puts them all in the one surface (a material value, or a name the library
# knows) with the plinth's middle on the floor at `at`. The ball is 0.5 m across; the plinth is
# round, 0.6 m across and 0.12 m high, one lathe, its top outer edge rounded at 5 mm and a round
# groove 1 cm wide and deep in its top round the ball (at a radius of 0.2 m, lips rounded at 2 mm);
# behind it (toward -z) a slab 2 by 2 m and 4 cm thick lies flat, to show the tile repeating and
# the shine at a low angle; beside it a 10 cm cube with 2 mm rounded edges shows the size.
# (A test rig, not a piece for a scene: sofa-sized in triangles, so the plinth is round, not 16-sided.)
# `loop library picture <surface>` uses it.
#
# kind: model
# place: test_ball("oak", AT)
# credit: J Production, made in Loop3D
# licence: our own work, free for every J Production project
# from: order M8 (2026-11-11)
# tags: test, surface
# size: sofa

to test_ball(surface, at)
  # the plinth: one round lathe, so its groove can gather dirt (marks never cross from one thing to
  # another). The outline goes from the bottom up; points every 2 to 4 cm on the flat stretches
  # either side of the groove keep its dirt from smearing across them.
  let outline = [vec(0.3, 0), vec(0.3, 0.115), vec(0.2985, 0.1185), vec(0.295, 0.12), vec(0.27, 0.12), vec(0.24, 0.12), vec(0.22, 0.12), vec(0.207, 0.12), vec(0.2056, 0.1194), vec(0.205, 0.118), vec(0.205, 0.112), vec(0.204, 0.11), vec(0.2, 0.11), vec(0.196, 0.11), vec(0.195, 0.112), vec(0.195, 0.118), vec(0.1956, 0.1194), vec(0.197, 0.12), vec(0.18, 0.12), vec(0.15, 0.12), vec(0.12, 0.12), vec(0.09, 0.12), vec(0.06, 0.12), vec(0.03, 0.12), vec(0, 0.12)]
  lathe profile: outline, segments: 96, at: at, material: surface
  # the ball, resting on the middle
  sphere 0.25, at: at + vec(0, 0.37, 0), material: surface
  # the slab, lying flat on the floor behind
  box size: vec(2, 0.04, 2), at: at + vec(0, 0.02, -1.4), material: surface
  # the cube, for size
  box size: vec(0.1, 0.1, 0.1), at: at + vec(0.6, 0.05, 0.1), material: surface, round: 0.002
